    <DataNode elementId="AT1G05680-MONOMER" textLabel="UDP-glycosyltransferase 74E2" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9SYK9" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="835" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">The UGT74E2 enzyme is a member of the group L class of glycosyltransferases found in Arabidopsis. Two other members of this family have been shown to conjugate glucose to phytohormones. UGT74E2 was tested against a number of different hormones, including |FRAME: INDOLE_ACETATE_AUXIN indole-3-acetate| and |FRAME: CPD-693 2-&lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt;-abscisate|, but it showed a strong preference for the auxin |FRAME: CPD-10507 indole-3-butyrate| (IBA) based on &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; assays. Furthermore, levels of |FRAME: CPD-10507 IBA| and |FRAME: CPD-12621 indole-3-butyryl-glucose| were up-regulated in plants over-expressing this enzyme demonstrating an endogenous role for UGT74E2 in IBA metabolism |CITS: [20798329]|. The transcript and protein levels for this enzyme are significantly elevated in response to |FRAME: HYDROGEN-PEROXIDE hydrogen peroxide|, suggesting that this enzyme may play a role in mediating crosstalk between ROS (reactive oxygen species) and auxin signaling pathways in plants |CITS: [20798329]|. Plants over-expressing UGT74E2 have increased tolerance to several stresses including elevated levels of NaCl and drought. These plants also have hypersensitivity to |FRAME: CPD-693 2-&lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt;-abscisate| in assays of seed germination and early seedling development |CITS: [20798329]|. UGT74E2 has also been shown to be active against several metabolites produced from the breakdown of the synthetic pollutant and explosive |FRAME: CPD-10455 4-amino-2,6-dinitrotoluene| (TNT). Although this does not represent an endogenous activity of the enzyme, it does suggest that UGT74E2 and other glycosyltransferases could play a role in the phytoremediation of dangerous substances |CITS: [18702669]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT1G05680-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT1G05680.1" />
