    <DataNode elementId="AT2G23260-MONOMER" textLabel="indole-3-acetic acid glucosyltransferase / abscisic acid glucosyltransferase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="O22182" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="750" centerY="835" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">Out of 10 putative UDP-glucosyltransferases from the Arabidopsis genome, only UGT84B1 showed high activity towards IAA in an in-vitro assay using purified recombinant proteins |CITS:[11042207]|. This protein was also shown to be able to catalyze the glucosylation of the naturally-occuring |FRAME:CPD-693|, as well as its &apos;unnatural&apos; counterpart: (-)-ABA |CITS:[LIM05]|. The significance of the dual glucosylation of this enzyme remains uncertain. When constitutively overexpressed &lt;i&gt;in planta&lt;/i&gt; the &lt;i&gt;UGT84B1&lt;/i&gt; gene was shown to be able to disturb IAA homeostasis |CITS:[11042207],[12445128 ]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT2G23260-MONOMER" />
