    <DataNode elementId="AT1G30530-MONOMER" textLabel="AT1G30530-MONOMER" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9S9P6" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="915" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">Using a functional genomics approach to identify candidate genes in mutant lines of Arabidopsis lacking the commonly found flavonol-3-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-rhamnoside-7-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-rhamnosides, the cDNA encoding UDP-rhamnose:flavonol-3-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-rhamnosyltransferase (UGT78D1) was isolated from &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt;. Its molecular function of attaching a rhamnosyl residue to the 3-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-position of the flavonols kaempferol and quercetin was confirmed by expressing and characterizing the recombinant protein in &lt;i&gt;E. coli&lt;/i&gt;. The glycosylation pattern obtained from UGT78D1 mutants and the preference of the 7-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucosyltransferase (UGT73C6) for glycosides rather than flavonol aglycons indicates that the 3-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-rhamnosylation is the first step in forming the predominant flavonol diglycosides (flavonol 3-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-rhamnoside-7-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-rhamnoside, flavonol 3-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-rhamnoside-7-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucoside) present in Arabidopsis|CITS: [12900416]|.</Comment>
